## Proof

Let `p = 1991 = 11·181`. Set `a = p^{1992}`, `b = p^{1990}`. Both `a,b` are odd (since `p` is odd), and `a = p²·b`. We seek

`A = (p−1)^a + (p+1)^b,`  and  `k = min(v_{11}(A), v_{181}(A))`.

Fix a prime `q | p` (so `q ∈ {11,181}`, hence `p ≡ 0 (mod q)`). Compute `v_q(A)`.

**Rewrite.** Since `a` is odd, `(p−1)^a = (−1)^a(1−p)^a = −(1−p)^a`. Also `(p+1)^b = (1+p)^b`. Thus

`A = −(1−p)^a + (1+p)^b.`

**Binomial expansion.**

`(1−p)^a = Σ_{j≥0} C(a,j)(−1)^j p^j`,  `(1+p)^b = Σ_{j≥0} C(b,j) p^j`,

`A = Σ_{j≥0} [ (−1)^{j+1} C(a,j) + C(b,j) ] p^j.`

- **j = 0:** `(−1)^1·1 + 1 = 0`.
- **j = 1:** `(a + b) p`.
- **j ≥ 2:** `[ (−1)^{j+1} C(a,j) + C(b,j) ] p^j`.

**The j = 1 term.**

`(a+b)p = (p^{1992}+p^{1990})p = p^{1991}(p²+1).`

Since `p ≡ 0 (mod q)`, we have `p²+1 ≡ 1 (mod q)`, so `v_q(p²+1) = 0`. Therefore

`v_q(j=1 term) = 1991.`

**The j ≥ 2 terms.** Because `v_q(a) = 1992` and `v_q(b) = 1990` are enormous, for every `1 ≤ i ≤ j−1` (with `j` modest) we have `a − i ≡ −i (mod q^{v_q(i)+1})` and `b − i ≡ −i (mod q^{v_q(i)+1})`, hence `v_q(a−i) = v_q(i)` and `v_q(b−i) = v_q(i)`. Therefore

`v_q(C(a,j)) = v_q(a) + Σ_{i=1}^{j−1} v_q(a−i) − v_q(j!) = 1992 + v_q((j−1)!) − v_q(j!) = 1992 − v_q(j),`

`v_q(C(b,j)) = 1990 − v_q(j).`

Since `1992 − v_q(j) > 1990 − v_q(j)`, the two summands `(−1)^{j+1}C(a,j)` and `C(b,j)` have **different** `q`-adic valuations, so they cannot cancel:

`v_q( (−1)^{j+1}C(a,j) + C(b,j) ) = 1990 − v_q(j).`

Multiplying by `p^j` (which contributes `j` to the valuation):

`v_q(j-th term) = 1990 + j − v_q(j).`

For `j ≥ 2`: since `q ≥ 2`, `v_q(j) ≤ log_2 j < j − 1`, so `j − v_q(j) ≥ 2`. Hence

`v_q(j-th term) ≥ 1990 + 2 = 1992 > 1991`  for all `j ≥ 2`.

**Conclusion of the valuation.** The `j = 1` term has `q`-adic valuation exactly `1991`, and every other term has valuation `≥ 1992`. No cancellation is possible (the minimum is achieved uniquely), so

`v_q(A) = 1991`  for each `q ∈ {11, 181}`.

**Final answer.**

`k = min(v_{11}(A), v_{181}(A)) = min(1991, 1991) = 1991.`

The largest positive integer `k` such that `1991^k | 1990^{1991^{1992}} + 1992^{1991^{1990}}` is

$$\boxed{k = 1991}.$$

